 normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-84d37eb1-f3c7-5886-b6e4-e84343d1696f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-84d37eb1-f3c7-5886-b6e4-e84343d1696f" :answer-field/id #uuid "84d37eb1-f3c7-5886-b6e4-e84343d1696f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dd9fc8b3-618d-5488-9e40-7e984f38b54b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-38beef69-795f-5e94-844c-2d988f85a1fa" "answer-341cebc5-0086-51c4-a7a7-568caded3433" "answer-dd9fc8b3-618d-5488-9e40-7e984f38b54b" "answer-4e9b4350-82ac-5374-a266-911f01cdc03d" "answer-c6087914-80dd-50e5-8ee5-7f3d56af6f93"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38beef69-795f-5e94-844c-2d988f85a1fa" :answer/id #uuid "38beef69-795f-5e94-844c-2d988f85a1fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-341cebc5-0086-51c4-a7a7-568caded3433" :answer/id #uuid "341cebc5-0086-51c4-a7a7-568caded3433" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd9fc8b3-618d-5488-9e40-7e984f38b54b" :answer/id #uuid "dd9fc8b3-618d-5488-9e40-7e984f38b54b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e9b4350-82ac-5374-a266-911f01cdc03d" :answer/id #uuid "4e9b4350-82ac-5374-a266-911f01cdc03d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6087914-80dd-50e5-8ee5-7f3d56af6f93" :answer/id #uuid "c6087914-80dd-50e5-8ee5-7f3d56af6f93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f1ac1f29-7051-5497-b0bb-1e7a6a92e87b" :question/id #uuid "f1ac1f29-7051-5497-b0bb-1e7a6a92e87b" :question/math-academy-id "q-149686" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4c47e5c7-7541-5b18-a52f-c2f68957f291"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4c47e5c7-7541-5b18-a52f-c2f68957f291" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c47e5c7-7541-5b18-a52f-c2f68957f291" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0e5a8fb6-b599-5596-9b2f-c97544575c60" :answer-field/choices ["answer-03a45c74-9b1e-5441-8919-df0ab8e1e247" "answer-08312884-c6ea-53f3-93c8-ff127f67b413" "answer-70327205-4e06-5633-84e8-ec7b8626d9c3" "answer-0e5a8fb6-b599-5596-9b2f-c97544575c60" "answer-426a15c7-aeeb-5693-ad27-b576c0204873"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03a45c74-9b1e-5441-8919-df0ab8e1e247" :answer/id #uuid "03a45c74-9b1e-5441-8919-df0ab8e1e247" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08312884-c6ea-53f3-93c8-ff127f67b413" :answer/id #uuid "08312884-c6ea-53f3-93c8-ff127f67b413" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70327205-4e06-5633-84e8-ec7b8626d9c3" :answer/id #uuid "70327205-4e06-5633-84e8-ec7b8626d9c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e5a8fb6-b599-5596-9b2f-c97544575c60" :answer/id #uuid "0e5a8fb6-b599-5596-9b2f-c97544575c60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-426a15c7-aeeb-5693-ad27-b576c0204873" :answer/id #uuid "426a15c7-aeeb-5693-ad27-b576c0204873" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b762e8ce-4cae-50c5-8387-1a9bdda932c1" :question/id #uuid "b762e8ce-4cae-50c5-8387-1a9bdda932c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-149687" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3c3fe428-01a8-5aff-b338-696798d0c3d5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3c3fe428-01a8-5aff-b338-696798d0c3d5" :answer-field/id #uuid "3c3fe428-01a8-5aff-b338-696798d0c3d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e2d51fd8-54c6-55fc-98e7-bbbf9e80a1a8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c1f71e57-2581-5569-a3a9-5c37294301bf" "answer-40d8306d-4b33-5cb2-8dc6-cfdc042ca82d" "answer-e2d51fd8-54c6-55fc-98e7-bbbf9e80a1a8" "answer-4d614b79-144b-5ba2-a516-545fad2f6222" "answer-7c0cf450-3b58-5d82-b9a3-ea1fd674b459"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1f71e57-2581-5569-a3a9-5c37294301bf" :answer/id #uuid "c1f71e57-2581-5569-a3a9-5c37294301bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40d8306d-4b33-5cb2-8dc6-cfdc042ca82d" :answer/id #uuid "40d8306d-4b33-5cb2-8dc6-cfdc042ca82d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2d51fd8-54c6-55fc-98e7-bbbf9e80a1a8" :answer/id #uuid "e2d51fd8-54c6-55fc-98e7-bbbf9e80a1a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d614b79-144b-5ba2-a516-545fad2f6222" :answer/id #uuid "4d614b79-144b-5ba2-a516-545fad2f6222" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c0cf450-3b58-5d82-b9a3-ea1fd674b459" :answer/id #uuid "7c0cf450-3b58-5d82-b9a3-ea1fd674b459" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-253331d9-b77f-57ef-b00e-dd4a38f9cd0d" :activity/id #uuid "253331d9-b77f-57ef-b00e-dd4a38f9cd0d" :activity/title "Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vectors in 2D" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050104 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/ste