/questions ["question-13eab2a4-15b8-5aae-888d-a5c1961fc009" "question-3f3094c1-9493-5020-b906-a8b3df41f18e" "question-e145a618-7b18-50b4-9616-c05db1f4edaa" "question-3ad65545-bf82-508f-8a9d-4bdd8ed0f623"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-deaa15dc-da8f-5e37-b1fc-ea24a0a7f029" :question/id #uuid "deaa15dc-da8f-5e37-b1fc-ea24a0a7f029" :question/math-academy-id "q-343191" :question/problem "![](images/69/69b4b3c2488cf394df687d7446ab5a49bab037de936402f02d43196f71dddf09.png)\n\nTwo glass plates are held a constant distance apart, forming a growing sheet of laminated glass resin. As rollers pull the plates apart, the length is increasing by $2\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ while the $1\\,\\text{cm}$ thickness does not change. If resin is injected so that the volume of the sheet is increasing at a rate of $32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min},$  how fast is its width changing when its dimensions are $10\\,\\text{cm}$ in length and $4\\,\\text{cm}$ in width?\n\nThe width is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =1\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=4\\,\\text{cm}$ and $z=1\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSince $z$ is not changing and $z=1,$ we get\n\n$$\nV=xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(xy) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =x(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+y(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32 & =10(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+4(2) \\\\ 32 & =10(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+8 \\\\ 24 & =10(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2.4\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is increasing at a rate of $2.4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=4\\,\\text{cm}$ and $z=1\\,\\text{cm}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-89f2717d-a35e-5e3f-aba4-917081b91c68"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89f2717d-a35e-5e3f-aba4-917081b91c68" :answer-field/id #uuid "89f2717d-a35e-5e3f-aba4-917081b91c68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4c4bd551-965b-5c88-811a-93ad01e05d86" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4c4bd551-965b-5c88-811a-93ad01e05d86"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c4bd551-965b-5c88-811a-93ad01e05d86" :answer/id #uuid "4c4bd551-965b-5c88-811a-93ad01e05d86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2dec6d3d-90b6-5bbb-9401-3e793424773e" :question/id #uuid "2dec6d3d-90b6-5bbb-9401-3e793424773e" :question/math-academy-id "q-343177" :question/problem "![](images/dd/dde167688f1693d34f07f89c923843643769369f767fec68e3578e910d8da3fb.png)\n\nA sugar cube is dissolving uniformly in water. If the volume of the cube decreases at a rate of $48\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},$ determine the rate at which the edges of the cube are decreasing when the volume of the cube is $8\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ Assume that the edge's rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe edges of the cube are decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side of the cube and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-48\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $V=8\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V=8\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 8 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=2$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-48$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -48 & =3(2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -48 & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the edges of the cube are decreasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=2\\,\\text{cm}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-67580179-a44d-55e9-9d4c-66e88565b431"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-67580179-a44d-55e9-9d4c-66e88565b431" :answer-field/id #uuid "67580179-a44d-55e9-9d4c-66e88565b431" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ed7400b8-da75-5dd3-ba2a-9ffd605e0895" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ed7400b8-da75-5dd3-ba2a-9ffd605e0895"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed7400b8-da75-5dd3-ba2a-9ffd605e0895" :answer/id #uuid "ed7400b8-da75-5dd3-ba2a-9ffd605e0895" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3a0ddd70-eefe-5886-baf8-3017327746b5" :question/id #uuid "3a0ddd70-eefe-5886-baf8-3017327746b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-30027" :question/problem "![](images/03/03e31c64b26e304e720ed0178ff23af8b2ca0955c678bd695e930e0a5cb9069a.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing by $0.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}$ and its height increasing at a rate of $1\\text{ feet/min}.$ If the volume of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $10{\\text{ feet}}^{3}\\text{/min},$  how fast is its width changing when its dimensions are $2\\text{ feet}$ in length, $3\\text{ feet}$ in width and $1\\text{ feet}$ in height?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\text{ feet}, \\\\ y & =1\\text{ feet}, \\\\ z & =3\\text{ feet}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-10{\\text{ feet}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=2\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=1\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=3\\,\\text{feet}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\cdot y\\cdot z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =xy(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+xz(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+yz(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =(2)(1)(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+(2)(3)(1)+(1)(3)(-0.5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-7.25\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is decreasing at a rate of $7.25\\,\\text{feet}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=2\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=1\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=3\\,\\text{feet}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e24d6138-77a7-51ac-903f-75f16a69daee"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e24d6138-77a7-51ac-903f-75f16a69daee" :answer-field/id #uuid "e24d6138-77a7-51ac-903f-75f16a69daee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4be5baa3-e1d0-576d-8ab9-af6042eedd71" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fce26c00-7a81-52cc-9e1d-4d762eae2284" "answer-4be5baa3-e1d0-576d-8ab9-af6042eedd71" "answer-2bcf9157-1f36-52e9-a61e-f8e687fb3456" "answer-a51d42a0-8eab-5b68-be60-237b93b5dc6e" "answer-9dde3e4d-67fd-59dc-b0bb-4064cd68c048"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fce26c00-7a81-52cc-9e1d-4d762eae2284" :answer/id #uuid "fce26c00-7a81-52cc-9e1d-4d762eae2284" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4be5baa3-e1d0-576d-8ab9-af6042eedd71" :answer/id #uuid "4be5baa3-e1d0-576d-8ab9-af6042eedd71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7.25\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bcf9157-1f36-52e9-a61e-f8e687fb3456" :answer/id #uuid "2bcf9157-1f36-52e9-a61e-f8e687fb3456" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a51d42a0-8eab-5b68-be60-237b93b5dc6e" :answer/id #uuid "a51d42a0-8eab-5b68-be60-237b93b5dc6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9dde3e4d-67fd-59dc-b0bb-4064cd68c048" :answer/id #uuid "9dde3e4d-67fd-59dc-b0bb-4064cd68c048" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9e7d3cb4-0661-5b5b-a138-b4024c524f85" :question/id #uuid "9e7d3cb4-0661-5b5b-a138-b4024c524f85" :question/math-academy-id "q-116066" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d2be88ec9cb7559ca8d80d528e143d4f2dd72109c9dc576b353dd352855c53ff.png)\n\nA cube is expanding uniformly at a rate of $135{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ How fast is each edge of the cube increasing when the volume of the cube is $125{\\text{ cm}}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=135\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $V=125\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V=125\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 125 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=5$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=135$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 135 & =3(5)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sides of the cube are increasing at a rate of $\\frac{9}{5}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=5\\,\\text{cm}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-09c4b278-6409-5796-a889-b0dd450f73c6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-09c4b278-6409-5796-a889-b0dd450f73c6" :answer-field/id #uuid "09c4b278-6409-5796-a889-b0dd450f73c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8237770d-ccc6-56ab-a4b5-c7878730f2ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-790db2f0-5257-5b8a-86e2-065177a863ad" "answer-4ab47776-0aac-5751-a91c-c1bc7c35d2d7" "answer-bd8fbffd-c5b1-58f2-af4a-dc00b4611c89" "answer-a04f8d85-00af-5c6c-b617-1c2abd0264df" "answer-8237770d-ccc6-56ab-a4b5-c7878730f2ea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-790db2f0-5257-5b8a-86e2-065177a863ad" :answer/id #uuid "790db2f0-5257-5b8a-86e2-065177a863ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ab47776-0aac-5751-a91c-c1bc7c35d2d7" :answer/id #uuid "4ab47776-0aac-5751-a91c-c1bc7c35d2d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd8fbffd-c5b1-58f2-af4a-dc00b4611c89" :answer/id #uuid "bd8fbffd-c5b1-58f2-af4a-dc00b4611c89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a04f8d85-00af-5c6c-b617-1c2abd0264df" :answer/id #uuid "a04f8d85-00af-5c6c-b617-1c2abd0264df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8237770d-ccc6-56ab-a4b5-c7878730f2ea" :answer/id #uuid "8237770d-ccc6-56ab-a4b5-c7878730f2ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b3878760-f553-50af-9094-65f6b89b14da" :question/id #uuid "b3878760-f553-50af-9094-65f6b89b14da" :question/math-academy-id "q-116266" :question/problem "![](images/00/0060643c716e8f9c6d0420c419ba1d6efd7c3dc986279feb4ceae9f860dfb313.png)\n\nThe length of the rectangular prism shown above is increasing, while the $4\\,\\text{in}$ width and the $6\\,\\text{in}$ height don't change. What is the rate of change of the length of the rectangular prism if the volume is increasing at a rate of $72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\,\\text{in}, \\\\ z & =6\\,\\text{in}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $y=4\\,\\text{in}$ and $z=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nV=x(4)(6)=24x.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(24x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =24(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known value into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72 & =24(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\,\\text{in}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the rectangular prism is increasing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-94705966-f953-5894-bfa7-9571835bf295"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-94705966-f953-5894-bfa7-9571835bf295" :answer-field/id #uuid "94705966-f953-5894-bfa7-9571835bf295" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f48115a9-c89d-51fa-b46a-7491efcdf077" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f48115a9-c89d-51fa-b46a-7491efcdf077" "answer-dd65f9e7-be1f-50f3-829c-d1434e73e451" "answer-4331b15a-72a2-5d98-9fc3-899e4d9a04fa" "answer-e9c3fe14-8f54-52bd-bfdb-9a39b3520805" "answer-9832c67a-2d5f-5d3a-8360-50943f6ba6d7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f48115a9-c89d-51fa-b46a-7491efcdf077" :answer/id #uuid "f48115a9-c89d-51fa-b46a-7491efcdf077" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd65f9e7-be1f-50f3-829c-d1434e73e451" :answer/id #uuid "dd65f9e7-be1f-50f3-829c-d1434e73e451" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4331b15a-72a2-5d98-9fc3-899e4d9a04fa" :answer/id #uuid "4331b15a-72a2-5d98-9fc3-899e4d9a04fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9c3fe14-8f54-52bd-bfdb-9a39b3520805" :answer/id #uuid "e9c3fe14-8f54-52bd-bfdb-9a39b3520805" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9832c67a-2d5f-5d3a-8360-50943f6ba6d7" :answer/id #uuid "9832c67a-2d5f-5d3a-8360-50943f6ba6d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-84586114-96d4-5e09-89f7-bf9841d73d0c" :question/id #uuid "84586114-96d4-5e09-89f7-bf9841d73d0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-314521" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8c4a2802cbd06159b0b0dcbee59f5b4a6e93b4c4e029dc88d04352c50471659e.png)\n\nAn elastic rectangular prism whose height is $15\\text{ in}$ and width is $24\\text{ in}$ is being stretched. If its length is increasing at a rate of $8\\text{ in/min},$ what is the rate of change of the surface area of the rectangular prism?\n\nThe surface area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. The variables $x,S,$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =15\\text{ in}, \\\\ z & =24\\text{ in}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =8\\text{ in}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nS=2(x)(24)+2x(15)+2(15)(24)=78x+720.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =78(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the above expression using $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=8,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =78(8)=624.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rectangular prism's surface area is increasing at a rate of $624\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fb6a5f66-96e9-5ca5-964a-29cec265540a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fb6a5f66-96e9-5ca5-964a-29cec265540a" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb6a5f66-96e9-5ca5-964a-29cec265540a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ad11d165-ba21-5fa4-bbce-11ab5622f378" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ad11d165-ba21-5fa4-bbce-11ab5622f378"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad11d165-ba21-5fa4-bbce-11ab5622f378" :answer/id #uuid "ad11d165-ba21-5fa4-bbce-11ab5622f378" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "624" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bacb6b56-6056-5c36-b7bd-9714bddbfa56" :question/id #uuid "bacb6b56-6056-5c36-b7bd-9714bddbfa56" :question/math-academy-id "q-49011" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f50ef86e6663531757afb15dac45705de30ccfaae4d7079bc5a9cf474b7467f7.png)\n\nIn a laboratory, a researcher is increasing the edges of an iron cube at a rate of $0.5\\text{ feet/min}.$ How fast is the surface area changing when each edge is $3\\text{ feet?}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.5\\,\\text{feet}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=3\\,\\text{feet}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(S) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12(3)(0.5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cube's surface area is changing at a rate of $18\\,{\\text{feet}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-01a6724f-08c0-556a-8d01-69c8d214a05a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-01a6724f-08c0-556a-8d01-69c8d214a05a" :answer-field/id #uuid "01a6724f-08c0-556a-8d01-69c8d214a05a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2c36ed08-005a-59b5-8732-844b9503b009" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8e8d429c-3e5b-567b-a273-b93fb30b35c3" "answer-381c9c71-7212-5a03-8354-5571704318c2" "answer-3d3a5042-2b9f-58d0-aa25-271eb4f26711" "answer-2c36ed08-005a-59b5-8732-844b9503b009" "answer-22f79d57-7057-5d04-9cfc-2b04e2a421f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e8d429c-3e5b-567b-a273-b93fb30b35c3" :answer/id #uuid "8e8d429c-3e5b-567b-a273-b93fb30b35c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10.5{\\text{ feet}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-381c9c71-7212-5a03-8354-5571704318c2" :answer/id #uuid "381c9c71-7212-5a03-8354-5571704318c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12{\\text{ feet}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d3a5042-2b9f-58d0-aa25-271eb4f26711" :answer/id #uuid "3d3a5042-2b9f-58d0-aa25-271eb4f26711" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15.5{\\text{ feet}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c36ed08-005a-59b5-8732-844b9503b009" :answer/id #uuid "2c36ed08-005a-59b5-8732-844b9503b009" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18{\\text{ feet}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22f79d57-7057-5d04-9cfc-2b04e2a421f1" :answer/id #uuid "22f79d57-7057-5d04-9cfc-2b04e2a421f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9{\\text{ feet}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-59e7f49b-ab56-5848-a273-6624c60395ee" :question/id #uuid "59e7f49b-ab56-5848-a273-6624c60395ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-343210" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0bea5354a71b12e82cba561901db0fc97de873eb6f9985f0deb15afba5da801.png)\n\nA cube-shaped sugar cube is dissolving uniformly in tea. If the surface area of the cube is decreasing at a rate of $24\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ at what rate are the edges changing when each edge is $2\\,\\text{cm}?$ Assume that each edge is decreasing at the same rate, and the rate of decrease of the edge is positive.\n\nThe cube's edge is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-24\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=2\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $x$ knowing the rate of change of $S.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(S) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -24 & =12(2)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the edges are decreasing at a rate of $1\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7ad2e690-1a98-50c7-805e-a491fbfad8c3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7ad2e690-1a98-50c7-805e-a491fbfad8c3" :answer-field/id #uuid "7ad2e690-1a98-50c7-805e-a491fbfad8c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4f5a0c29-6e4d-5a80-af9f-64a6b2caa904" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4f5a0c29-6e4d-5a80-af9f-64a6b2caa904"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f5a0c29-6e4d-5a80-af9f-64a6b2caa904" :answer/id #uuid "4f5a0c29-6e4d-5a80-af9f-64a6b2caa904" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-13a313ba-f3f4-5888-a2ed-231c29e8a4fa" :question/id #uuid "13a313ba-f3f4-5888-a2ed-231c29e8a4fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-343310" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4723361163d4ca372019a91a347d2cb3d0aeee0ece1ace62762348dede9cea8.png)\n\nA worker is coating a surface with epoxy using a thickness gauge, which fixes the epoxy height at $5\\,\\text{in}.$ If the length is increasing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}$ and the width is increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s},$ how fast is the surface area of the epoxy layer increasing when its length is $12\\,\\text{in}$ and its width is $7\\,\\text{in}?$\n\nThe surface area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $S$ be its surface area. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =12\\,\\text{in}, \\\\ y & =7\\,\\text{in}, \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{in}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x$ and $y.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSince $z=5\\,\\text{in}$ is not changing, we get\n\n$$\nS=10x+2xy+10y.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10x+2xy+10y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(10+2x)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(10+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(10+2\\cdot 12)(2)+(10+2\\cdot 7)(3) \\\\  & =(34)(2)+(24)(3) \\\\  & =68+72 \\\\  & =140\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the rectangular prism is increasing at a rate of $140\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-12874409-02a6-5ec7-bc52-1cd515da4b29"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-12874409-02a6-5ec7-bc52-1cd515da4b29" :answer-field/id #uuid "12874409-02a6-5ec7-bc52-1cd515da4b29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-89474594-05e8-543d-b7ea-9e4daa46e36d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-89474594-05e8-543d-b7ea-9e4daa46e36d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89474594-05e8-543d-b7ea-9e4daa46e36d" :answer/id #uuid "89474594-05e8-543d-b7ea-9e4daa46e36d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "140" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7c0a4827-45c4-5e42-a7d2-ab4337115042" :question/id #uuid "7c0a4827-45c4-5e42-a7d2-ab4337115042" :question/math-academy-id "q-116486" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f87cf02aac4cee2680b2045b13fc2e7ce006c39c37fa470a7803c56624d6acd.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ the width is increasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ and the $6\\,\\text{cm}$ height does not change. How fast is the surface area of the rectangular prism decreasing when its dimensions are $4\\,\\text{cm}$ in length and $11\\,\\text{cm}$ in width?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $S$ be its surface area. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =11\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =6\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x$ and $y.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSince $z=6\\,\\text{cm}$ is not changing, we get\n\n$$\nS=12x+2xy+12y.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12x+2xy+12y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2x+12)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(12+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2\\cdot 4+12)(5)+(12+2\\cdot 11)(-4) \\\\  & =100-136 \\\\  & =-36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8155f6ea-7ad1-5d0c-9428-1db9bd0cca29"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8155f6ea-7ad1-5d0c-9428-1db9bd0cca29" :answer-field/id #uuid "8155f6ea-7ad1-5d0c-9428-1db9bd0cca29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ed69a781-7c92-5ec0-b5f9-cb723c492b54" :answer-field/choices ["answer-90ecd540-4787-5529-b79c-562d35ce6e76" "answer-eedee9f0-a7c7-5bdf-8d5b-a60ae7bd02f7" "answer-6c74f04b-bcfa-5467-9414-e11ffa53e63f" "answer-26ce561d-4554-50ea-b082-6ff5e35c97e1" "answer-ed69a781-7c92-5ec0-b5f9-cb723c492b54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90ecd540-4787-5529-b79c-562d35ce6e76" :answer/id #uuid "90ecd540-4787-5529-b79c-562d35ce6e76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eedee9f0-a7c7-5bdf-8d5b-a60ae7bd02f7" :answer/id #uuid "eedee9f0-a7c7-5bdf-8d5b-a60ae7bd02f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c74f04b-bcfa-5467-9414-e11ffa53e63f" :answer/id #uuid "6c74f04b-bcfa-5467-9414-e11ffa53e63f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "34\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26ce561d-4554-50ea-b082-6ff5e35c97e1" :answer/id #uuid "26ce561d-4554-50ea-b082-6ff5e35c97e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "35\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed69a781-7c92-5ec0-b5f9-cb723c492b54" :answer/id #uuid "ed69a781-7c92-5ec0-b5f9-cb723c492b54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-13eab2a4-15b8-5aae-888d-a5c1961fc009" :question/id #uuid "13eab2a4-15b8-5aae-888d-a5c1961fc009" :question/math-academy-id "q-343334" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac1e157b59bb632068e45776cdeb6bdc2dee42fd7f9b92ee2ea61401c1087d29.png)\n\nA rectangular block of ice is melting in a narrow channel that keeps its length fixed at $12\\,\\text{in}.$ Its width is decreasing by $1\\,\\text{in}\\text{/h},$ while its height is decreasing by $0.4\\,\\text{in}\\text{/h}.$ How fast is its volume decreasing when its dimensions are $5\\,\\text{in}$ in width and $4\\,\\text{in}$ in height? Assume that the volume's rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe volume is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" :question/worked-solution "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L,$ $W,$ and $H$ be the length, width, and height of the ice block, respectively, and\n\n- let $V$ be the volume of the ice block.\n\nNote that $H,$ $W,$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ is constant. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=12\\text{ in},\\, & W=5\\text{ in}, \\\\ H=4\\text{ in},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-1\\text{ in/h}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-0.4\\text{ in/h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the three time-dependent variables, $V,$ $W$ and $H,$ is given by the formula for the volume of ice block:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ treating $L$ as a constant, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(WH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L[W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t})+H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t})] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L\\cdot W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(12)(5)(-0.4)+(12)(4)(-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-24+(-48) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of ice is decreasing at a rate of $72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e3b46e6b-bbfb-526b-9cc5-0bb7578fda4b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e3b46e6b-bbfb-526b-9cc5-0bb7578fda4b" :answer-field/id #uuid "e3b46e6b-bbfb-526b-9cc5-0bb7578fda4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9f7bc959-ffc0-515f-8e2d-91ce2f38383f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9f7bc959-ffc0-515f-8e2d-91ce2f38383f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f7bc959-ffc0-515f-8e2d-91ce2f38383f" :answer/id #uuid "9f7bc959-ffc0-515f-8e2d-91ce2f38383f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "72" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3f3094c1-9493-5020-b906-a8b3df41f18e" :question/id #uuid "3f3094c1-9493-5020-b906-a8b3df41f18e" :question/math-academy-id "q-30020" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f84ba9d0dbbf8f10fc3244c5ca3a79d4d619ab2360ac5b77fe88ddc0943dd68f.png)\n\nA rectangular swimming pool (with a horizontal bottom) is being drained. If its length and width are $25\\text{ m}$ and $15\\text{ m}$, respectively, and the water level is falling at a rate of $0.25\\,\\text{m/min},$ how fast is the water draining out of the pool?" :question/worked-solution "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the swimming pool, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=25\\,\\text{m},\\,W=15\\,\\text{m},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-0.25\\,\\text{m}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of water:\n\n$$\nV=LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWh$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(25)(15)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =375\\cdot (-0.25) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-93.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water is draining out at a rate of $93.75\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f82c44d4-53de-55bc-86e0-85f055a9f683"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f82c44d4-53de-55bc-86e0-85f055a9f683" :answer-field/id #uuid "f82c44d4-53de-55bc-86e0-85f055a9f683" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-896f763d-06f2-5ad2-a422-baec40f25127" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ce363e11-7033-5f84-a491-d1ab09cce555" "answer-8b2fd06e-ebdc-5efb-9f47-fcfd77c60ccd" "answer-d0b90a68-307d-55af-925d-ba90f1e97c00" "answer-896f763d-06f2-5ad2-a422-baec40f25127" "answer-e0490112-c171-5687-8b4a-c7699a51cb6e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce363e11-7033-5f84-a491-d1ab09cce555" :answer/id #uuid "ce363e11-7033-5f84-a491-d1ab09cce555" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "105\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b2fd06e-ebdc-5efb-9f47-fcfd77c60ccd" :answer/id #uuid "8b2fd06e-ebdc-5efb-9f47-fcfd77c60ccd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "115\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0b90a68-307d-55af-925d-ba90f1e97c00" :answer/id #uuid "d0b90a68-307d-55af-925d-ba90f1e97c00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "90\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-896f763d-06f2-5ad2-a422-baec40f25127" :answer/id #uuid "896f763d-06f2-5ad2-a422-baec40f25127" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "93.75\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0490112-c171-5687-8b4a-c7699a51cb6e" :answer/id #uuid "e0490112-c171-5687-8b4a-c7699a51cb6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "98.15\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e145a618-7b18-50b4-9616-c05db1f4edaa" :question/id #uuid "e145a618-7b18-50b4-9616-c05db1f4edaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-343322" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e700536730a443e47e56bf33cd7dcb460032b72984d5aaaaf75d1de987c6b495.png)\n\nA factory uses a rectangular mold with a fixed width of $18\\,\\text{in}$ and a fixed height of $10\\,\\text{in}.$ Liquid resin is poured in so that the resin fills the mold and the length increases at a rate of $3\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}$ as the front face moves. What is the rate of change of the resin's volume in the mold?\n\nThe resin's volume is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $H$ and $W$ be the height and width of the rectangular mold, respectively, and\n\n- let $L$ and $V$ be the length and volume of the resin, respectively.\n\nNote that $L$ and $V$ change with time, while $H$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nH=10\\,\\text{in},\\,W=18\\,\\text{in},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $L,$ is given by the formula for the volume of resin:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $H$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =WH\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(18)(10)\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =180\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =540.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resin's volume is increasing at a rate of $540\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-996f61b9-bb6c-58a3-b6ed-c482bde482da"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-996f61b9-bb6c-58a3-b6ed-c482bde482da" :answer-field/id #uuid "996f61b9-bb6c-58a3-b6ed-c482bde482da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-637d44e3-fc00-59b5-9b83-bb2c15f53289" :answer-field/choices ["answer-637d44e3-fc00-59b5-9b83-bb2c15f53289"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-637d44e3-fc00-59b5-9b83-bb2c15f53289" :answer/id #uuid "637d44e3-fc00-59b5-9b83-bb2c15f53289" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "540" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3ad65545-bf82-508f-8a9d-4bdd8ed0f623" :question/id #uuid "3ad65545-bf82-508f-8a9d-4bdd8ed0f623" :question/math-academy-id "q-343323" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b0876986e02941b588bf9bacbc5a0caa61eb2b95021fa4ed52408dd4f5d3f72.png)\n\nA rectangular block of clay is being reshaped as it moves through a press. Its height is decreasing at a rate of $0.5\\,\\text{ft/min},$ its width is increasing at a rate of $1\\,\\text{ ft/min},$ and its length is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{ft/min}.$ How fast is its volume changing when its dimensions are $10\\,\\text{ft}$ in length, $4\\,\\text{ft}$ in width, and $3\\,\\text{ft}$ in height? Assume that the rate of decrease of volume is positive.\n\nThe volume of the rectangular block of clay is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $L,$ $W,$ $H$ and $V$ be the length, the width, the height, and the volume of the box, respectively. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=10\\text{ ft},\\, & W=4\\text{ ft}, \\\\ H=3\\text{ ft},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\text{ ft/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=1\\text{ ft/min},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-0.5\\text{ ft/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the four variables is given by the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ using the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(4)(3)(-2)+(10)(3)(1)+(10)(4)(-0.5) \\\\  & =-14{\\text{ ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the rectangular block of clay is decreasing at a rate of $14{\\text{ ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2e3e231d-940f-56e2-b89e-9c44df37d8f2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2e3e231d-940f-56e2-b89e-9c44df37d8f2" :answer-field/id #uuid "2e3e231d-940f-56e2-b89e-9c44df37d8f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7d285a60-7326-5698-83d2-37558960d4c2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7d285a60-7326-5698-83d2-37558960d4c2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d285a60-7326-5698-83d2-37558960d4c2" :answer/id #uuid "7d285a60-7326-5698-83d2-37558960d4c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064314 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-62924b2e-7117-58f6-a2b2-963583643766"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064507 :question/problem "| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n=30$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.2).$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2389\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ | $\\,0.2327\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2709\\,$ | $\\,0.2676\\,$ | $\\,0.2643\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.3050\\,$ | $\\,0.3015\\,$ | $\\,0.2981\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=30,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 0.3+(-1)\\cdot 0.2+0\\cdot 0.1+2\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =-0.6-0.2+0+0.8 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =((-2)^{2}\\cdot 0.3+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{0}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.4)-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(1.2+0.2+0+1.6)-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(0,\\frac{3}{30}) \\\\  & ∼N(0,0.1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.2) & =P(\\overset{―}{X}<-0.2)+P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.2) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<\\frac{-0.2-0}{\\sqrt{0.1}})+P(Z>\\frac{0.2-0}{\\sqrt{0.1}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-0.63)+P(Z>0.63) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.63)+1-P(Z<0.63) \\\\  & =1+Φ(-0.63)-Φ(0.63).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.63)=0.2643.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, by the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\,Φ(0.63)=1-Φ(-0.63).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.2) & =1+Φ(-0.63)-Φ(0.63) \\\\  & =2Φ(-0.63) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.2643 \\\\  & =0.5286.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186061272 :answer-field/correct 17592186061275 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-62924b2e-7117-58f6-a2b2-963583643766" :question/id #uuid "62924b2e-7117-58f6-a2b2-963583643766" :question/math-academy-id "q-143515" :question/problem "| $x$ | $-8$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.3$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{60}$ be a random sample of size $n=60$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is less than $-4.5.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=60,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot 0.3+(-4)\\cdot 0.3+(-2)\\cdot 0.3+2\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =-2.4-1.2-0.6+0.2 \\\\  & =-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =((-8)^{2}\\cdot 0.3+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 0.3+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 0.3+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-(-4)^{2} \\\\  & =(19.2+4.8+1.2+0.4)-16 \\\\  & =9.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(-4,\\frac{9.6}{60}) \\\\  & ∼N(-4,0.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.5).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.5) & ≈P(Z<\\frac{-4.5-(-4)}{\\sqrt{0.16}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.5)=Φ(-1.25).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e3d35207-52bf-5e87-8342-86fa07643744"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e3d35207-52bf-5e87-8342-86fa07643744" :answer-field/id #uuid "e3d35207-52bf-5e87-8342-86fa07643744" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8465315b-a190-5203-b11a-3e21ec72ee2c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2a41c234-f9c9-5382-8487-7886f440fe59" "answer-122e6ac5-9cff-55b6-b18d-000a481d0709" "answer-a173100e-d855-5223-a3de-e706ed5de992" "answer-8465315b-a190-5203-b11a-3e21ec72ee2c" "answer-a4cbe218-05ef-50ce-b350-172a085d5edd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a41c234-f9c9-5382-8487-7886f440fe59" :answer/id #uuid "2a41c234-f9c9-5382-8487-7886f440fe59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(-0.5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-122e6ac5-9cff-55b6-b18d-000a481d0709" :answer/id #uuid "122e6ac5-9cff-55b6-b18d-000a481d0709" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(-0.75)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a173100e-d855-5223-a3de-e706ed5de992" :answer/id #uuid "a173100e-d855-5223-a3de-e706ed5de992" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8465315b-a190-5203-b11a-3e21ec72ee2c" :answer/id #uuid "8465315b-a190-5203-b11a-3e21ec72ee2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(-1.25)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4cbe218-05ef-50ce-b350-172a085d5edd" :answer/id #uuid "a4cbe218-05ef-50ce-b350-172a085d5edd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(-1.5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-8986df93-470d-5192-a0f6-95b5da54c771" :activity/id #uuid "8986df93-470d-5192-a0f6-95b5da54c771" :activity/title "The Central Limit Theorem" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049014 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-090de2d7-fdb9-59df-bf2e-1d83a01726ae" "step-f80fbbff-d539-54c2-a972-4e6c9ba72aed" "step-13369923-3f38-528e-a662-06a089334d9c" "step-03b3cfb5-03c9-54cc-adb7-554c97f349a2" "step-cffb3e05-e1d3-55d2-9fb4-366dc20dfe0e"] :activity/first-step "step-090de2d7-fdb9-59df-bf2e-1d83a01726ae" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-090de2d7-fdb9-59df-bf2e-1d83a01726ae" :step/id #uuid "090de2d7-fdb9-59df-bf2e-1d83a01726ae" :step/content 17592186499566 :step/next "step-f80fbbff-d539-54c2-a972-4e6c9ba72aed" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f80fbbff-d539-54c2-a972-4e6c9ba72aed" :step/id #uuid "f80fbbff-d539-54c2-a972-4e6c9ba72aed" :step/content 17592186499537 :step/next "step-13369923-3f38-528e-a662-06a089334d9c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-13369923-3f38-528e-a662-06a089334d9c" :step/id #uuid "13369923-3f38-528e-a662-06a089334d9c" :step/content 17592186064507 :step/next "step-03b3cfb5-03c9-54cc-adb7-554c97f349a2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-03b3cfb5-03c9-54cc-adb7-554c97f349a2" :step/id #uuid "03b3cfb5-03c9-54cc-adb7-554c97f349a2" :step/content 17592186519526 :step/next "step-cffb3e05-e1d3-55d2-9fb4-366dc20dfe0e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cffb3e05-e1d3-55d2-9fb4-366dc20dfe0e" :step/id #uuid "cffb3e05-e1d3-55d2-9fb4-366dc20dfe0e" :step/content "question-62924b2e-7117-58f6-a2b2-963583643766" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087755 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-35496066-8708-503f-bd5d-57e7d1387c52" "question-80ca9b7a-fc1f-5555-a096-84f9c05d5b17"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087571 :question/problem "Determine if $x+3$ is a factor of $p(x)=x^3+3x^2-3x-9.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the factor theorem, $(x-r)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x = r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, then, $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x = -3$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo, we just need to check if $x=-3$ is a root of $p(x).$ In other words, we need to check if $p(-3)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+3(-3)^{2}-3(-3)-9 \\\\  & =-27+27+9-9 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, $p(-3)=0,$ so $x = -3$ is a root of the polynomial. Consequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $p(x).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087756 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dd794f4b-e202-5786-ad78-bc211e098112" "question-3e0ff1de-8233-57fb-9fb2-58728b6e261f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087572 :question/problem "Determine if $(4-3x)$ is a factor of $p(x)=-6x^2+11x-4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the factor theorem, $(ax-b)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x = \\dfrac{b}{a}$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(4-3x).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-3x & =0 \\\\ 4 & =3x \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, then, $(4-3x)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is also a root of $p(x).$\n\nSo, we just need to check if $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is a root of $p(x).$ In other words, we need to check if $p\\left( \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) =0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\frac{4}{3}) & =-6{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+11(\\frac{4}{3})-4 \\\\  & =-6\\cdot \\frac{16}{9}+\\frac{44}{3}-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{-32+44-12}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, $p \\left( \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)=0,$ so $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is a root of the polynomial. Consequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(4-3x)$ is indeed a factor of $p(x).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-35496066-8708-503f-bd5d-57e7d1387c52" :question/id #uuid "35496066-8708-503f-bd5d-57e7d1387c52" :question/math-academy-id "q-112455" :question/problem "For which of the following polynomials is $(x-2)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x-10$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{3}+8$\n\nIII. $h(x)={x}^{3}+4x-16$" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=2$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=2$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=2$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & ={2}^{2}+3(2)-10 \\\\  & =4+6-10 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x-2)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=2$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={2}^{3}+8 \\\\  & =8+8 \\\\  & =16\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is not a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=2$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(2) & ={2}^{3}+4(2)-16 \\\\  & =8+8-16 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x-2)$ is indeed a factor of $h(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-410d3b37-dbfa-5b15-aa19-c40da8cea6c7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-410d3b37-dbfa-5b15-aa19-c40da8cea6c7" :answer-field/id #uuid "410d3b37-dbfa-5b15-aa19-c40da8cea6c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a4c1532d-208d-51db-bb26-0e792b6f66e3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c05ab628-4248-5c64-bd22-1434aff7961e" "answer-a4c1532d-208d-51db-bb26-0e792b6f66e3" "answer-87ee6efa-aeba-5dd5-bf88-cb72af28c2be" "answer-917aaf23-2a71-5c96-9781-e7bc343926d7" "answer-2120844b-39e0-50a0-83b7-91462ed37227"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c05ab628-4248-5c64-bd22-1434aff7961e" :answer/id #uuid "c05ab628-4248-5c64-bd22-1434aff7961e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4c1532d-208d-51db-bb26-0e792b6f66e3" :answer/id #uuid "a4c1532d-208d-51db-bb26-0e792b6f66e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87ee6efa-aeba-5dd5-bf88-cb72af28c2be" :answer/id #uuid "87ee6efa-aeba-5dd5-bf88-cb72af28c2be" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-917aaf23-2a71-5c96-9781-e7bc343926d7" :answer/id #uuid "917aaf23-2a71-5c96-9781-e7bc343926d7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2120844b-39e0-50a0-83b7-91462ed37227" :answer/id #uuid "2120844b-39e0-50a0-83b7-91462ed37227" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-80ca9b7a-fc1f-5555-a096-84f9c05d5b17" :question/id #uuid "80ca9b7a-fc1f-5555-a096-84f9c05d5b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-73772" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{2}+2x-3?$\n\nI. $x=-3$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $x=-3$ is a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =(-3)^{2}+2(-3)-3 \\\\  & =9-6-3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-3$ is a root of $f(x),$ then $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ then $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4a98c68f-f943-512d-913b-2ea65a17cea7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4a98c68f-f943-512d-913b-2ea65a17cea7" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a98c68f-f943-512d-913b-2ea65a17cea7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-889ddc5d-2c8b-58e4-bef6-fa1b4225c168" :answer-field/choices ["answer-11d140f3-1414-532b-a8c7-d796e0fb4c38" "answer-0c4880bd-d3bf-5682-b1f3-41c300f391e7" "answer-889ddc5d-2c8b-58e4-bef6-fa1b4225c168" "answer-56772dec-1569-512c-953e-14ff78e36da6" "answer-08e54810-6a24-5857-8888-1cb2a6673578"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11d140f3-1414-532b-a8c7-d796e0fb4c38" :answer/id #uuid "11d140f3-1414-532b-a8c7-d796e0fb4c38" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c4880bd-d3bf-5682-b1f3-41c300f391e7" :answer/id #uuid "0c4880bd-d3bf-5682-b1f3-41c300f391e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-889ddc5d-2c8b-58e4-bef6-fa1b4225c168" :answer/id #uuid "889ddc5d-2c8b-58e4-bef6-fa1b4225c168" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56772dec-1569-512c-953e-14ff78e36da6" :answer/id #uuid "56772dec-1569-512c-953e-14ff78e36da6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08e54810-6a24-5857-8888-1cb2a6673578" :answer/id #uuid "08e54810-6a24-5857-8888-1cb2a6673578" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dd794f4b-e202-5786-ad78-bc211e098112" :question/id #uuid "dd794f4b-e202-5786-ad78-bc211e098112" :question/math-academy-id "q-112486" :question/problem "For which of the following polynomials is $(5x+4)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}$\n\nII. $g(x)=15{x}^{2}+25x+4$\n\nIII. $h(x)=4{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the factor theorem, $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(5x+4).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+4 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, then, $(5x+4)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if $x=-\\frac{4}{5}$ is a root of $p(x).$\n\nNow we will check if $x=-\\frac{4}{5}$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=-\\frac{4}{5}$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{4}{5}) & =5{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{3}+4{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =5(-\\frac{64}{125})+4(\\frac{16}{25}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{64}{25}+\\frac{64}{25} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(5x+4)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=-\\frac{4}{5}$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-\\frac{4}{5}) & =15{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}+25(-\\frac{4}{5})+4 \\\\  & =15(\\frac{16}{25})-20+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{5}-16 \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{5}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the factor theorem, $(5x+4)$ is not a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=-\\frac{4}{5}$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-\\frac{4}{5}) & =4{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{3}+5{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{64}{125})+5(\\frac{16}{25}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{256}{125}+\\frac{16}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{125}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the factor theorem, $(5x+4)$ is not a factor of $g(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bae3461d-fd61-5c28-bb95-fe22bb6cd076"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bae3461d-fd61-5c28-bb95-fe22bb6cd076" :answer-field/id #uuid "bae3461d-fd61-5c28-bb95-fe22bb6cd076" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2394e902-5877-5810-8394-10ee68af9948" :answer-field/choices ["answer-feea11ca-e2b0-5234-8225-36492bbaedf5" "answer-eb6d1d0b-251e-586b-a73a-66b5615b60f8" "answer-2394e902-5877-5810-8394-10ee68af9948" "answer-340065a2-9812-543e-996f-7f17091e976f" "answer-7b0a9111-8e94-5364-bc20-efc06aaf2f75"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-feea11ca-e2b0-5234-8225-36492bbaedf5" :answer/id #uuid "feea11ca-e2b0-5234-8225-36492bbaedf5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb6d1d0b-251e-586b-a73a-66b5615b60f8" :answer/id #uuid "eb6d1d0b-251e-586b-a73a-66b5615b60f8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2394e902-5877-5810-8394-10ee68af9948" :answer/id #uuid "2394e902-5877-5810-8394-10ee68af9948" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-340065a2-9812-543e-996f-7f17091e976f" :answer/id #uuid "340065a2-9812-543e-996f-7f17091e976f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b0a9111-8e94-5364-bc20-efc06aaf2f75" :answer/id #uuid "7b0a9111-8e94-5364-bc20-efc06aaf2f75" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3e0ff1de-8233-57fb-9fb2-58728b6e261f" :question/id #uuid "3e0ff1de-8233-57fb-9fb2-58728b6e261f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112465" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-25?$\n\nI. $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(2x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(2x-5)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(2x-5).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{5}{2}) & =4{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-25 \\\\  & =4(\\frac{25}{4})-25 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the factor theorem, since $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then $(2x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $(2x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ then $(2x-5)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-789b2306-2fd7-57dc-bf7b-7225dc82a697"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-789b2306-2fd7-57dc-bf7b-7225dc82a697" :answer-field/id #uuid "789b2306-2fd7-57dc-bf7b-7225dc82a697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-619e746c-8ca9-559a-ba02-5e619348d205" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7ec0dca9-31ae-5610-aaf5-c02dc410d7fe" "answer-0a5b5a30-e0e7-5744-b422-7047faa2e338" "answer-619e746c-8ca9-559a-ba02-5e619348d205" "answer-a1c39bb2-9093-5603-adeb-9895d5238cf8" "answer-962be32f-d335-5bd3-b444-dcb1f56ce30c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ec0dca9-31ae-5610-aaf5-c02dc410d7fe" :answer/id #uuid "7ec0dca9-31ae-5610-aaf5-c02dc410d7fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a5b5a30-e0e7-5744-b422-7047faa2e338" :answer/id #uuid "0a5b5a30-e0e7-5744-b422-7047faa2e338" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-619e746c-8ca9-559a-ba02-5e619348d205" :answer/id #uuid "619e746c-8ca9-559a-ba02-5e619348d205" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1c39bb2-9093-5603-adeb-9895d5238cf8" :answer/id #uuid "a1c39bb2-9093-5603-adeb-9895d5238cf8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-962be32f-d335-5bd3-b444-dcb1f56ce30c" :answer/id #uuid "962be32f-d335-5bd3-b444-dcb1f56ce30c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088933 :tutorial/content "If we know a *root* of a polynomial, we can immediately determine a *factor* of the polynomial using the **factor theorem**. \n\nThe factor theorem states that if $p(x)$ is a polynomial and $x={\\color{blue}{r}}$ is a *root* of $p(x),$ meaning that \n\n$$\np({\\color{blue}{r}})=0\n$$\n\nthen $(x-{\\color{blue}{r}})$ is a *factor* of $p(x).$\n\nThe converse of the factor theorem is also true. In other words, if $(x-{\\color{blue}{r}})$ is a factor of $p(x),$ then $x={\\color{blue}{r}}$ is a root of the polynomial.\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $p(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\np(x)=x^3-2x^2-x+2.\n$$\n\nNote that $x = {\\color{blue}2}$ is a root of this polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(2) & ={2}^{3}-2(2)^{2}-2+2 \\\\  & =8-8-2+2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to the factor theorem, $(x-{\\color{blue}2})$ is a *factor* of the polynomial. Indeed, it can be shown that this polynomial can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-x+2 & =(x-2)({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll learn more about factoring polynomials using the factor theorem in future lessons. But for now, we can check that this is correct by multiplying the parentheses." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088995 :tutorial/content "So, we know that the polynomial $p(x) = x^3-2x^2-x+2$ can be factored as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-x+2 & =(x-2)({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy dividing both sides by $(x-2),$ we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-2x^2-x+2}{x-2} = x^2-1.\n$$\n\nSince the result of dividing the polynomial $p(x)$ by the factor $({x-{\\color{black}2}})$ gives *another* polynomial, namely $x^2-1,$ we say that $({x-{\\color{black}2}})$ divides $p(x)$ *with no remainder*. \n\nWe can confirm that we get a remainder of zero when $p(x)$ is divided by $({x-{\\color{blue}2}})$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ |" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088934 :tutorial/content "We can also apply the factor theorem to factors of the form $(ax-b).$\n\nFor example, suppose that $(2x-3)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x).$ Then, we can immediately obtain a root of $p(x)$ by setting the factor equal to $0$ and solving for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the factor theorem, it follows that \n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe can summarize this more general form of the factor theorem as follows:\n\n- If $({\\color{red}a}x-{\\color{blue}b})$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x),$ then $x=\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}b}}{{\\color{red}a}}$ is a root of $p(x).$\n\n- Conversely, if $x=\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}b}}{{\\color{red}a}}$ is a root of $p(x),$ then $({\\color{red}a}x-{\\color{blue}b})$ is a factor of $p(x).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087917 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166849 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-884aeeae-3d24-5d63-bf46-362bb4b6d4a9" "question-e9b91c35-32fc-5eb1-b159-6a8e41b29e96" "question-92bd4292-ffa2-5865-9aeb-f65630d4d79e" "question-062302ea-c279-555c-ae6b-c50720d77be2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166850 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7838b043-50f3-55a8-b1f4-349f8ab97093" "question-4fe71dad-9ede-5ba8-b760-d7b3b5dd3ac6" "question-74a09e82-c6f4-5ba5-932c-01b4112c68fd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166743 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f5454beb-e917-5a8e-86e1-4e0232e2e094" "question-d7bc91a9-28bf-5e94-8b54-f2a8632dbd6e" "question-0ba278eb-bd8e-5c0e-8df1-e6e393201d18"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166744 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d0b4bd1c-2214-5d30-8eda-6058d4c56287" "question-befc2ac4-e448-5384-bd13-1313e616793b" "question-729d63e1-69ab-5f12-b423-6126fc288807"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-884aeeae-3d24-5d63-bf46-362bb4b6d4a9" :question/id #uuid "884aeeae-3d24-5d63-bf46-362bb4b6d4a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-105987" :question/problem "Given that $P(S|T)=0.3,$ $P(S)=0.6,$ and $P(T)=0.5,$ compute $P(T|S).$" :question/worked-solution "Using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T|S) & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.3)(0.5)}{0.6} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.15}{0.6} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fa4911c0-8b9b-5ded-8541-7a649e780685"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fa4911c0-8b9b-5ded-8541-7a649e780685" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4911c0-8b9b-5ded-8541-7a649e780685" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e357c35e-053f-5ac0-a631-7cb3fbe107da" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e357c35e-053f-5ac0-a631-7cb3fbe107da" "answer-b426b5ed-504c-537d-8c67-d362dc4b39ea" "answer-7b2ba900-5841-5c9