 {:db/id "answer-field-88c05edb-27ce-5999-bea1-f451f6da5f5a" :answer-field/id #uuid "88c05edb-27ce-5999-bea1-f451f6da5f5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-609ca3c1-35ba-50ca-a3f5-8ffaec27d898" :answer-field/choices ["answer-27570a30-bc64-5dd2-bf7a-c9dfffda49a0" "answer-3e970e43-56e8-51a7-8b67-1a34c8189d31" "answer-654e9075-d5c0-5942-8335-3af4eecc1069" "answer-03a4f702-63d6-559a-a0dd-ab624a5eb606" "answer-609ca3c1-35ba-50ca-a3f5-8ffaec27d898"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27570a30-bc64-5dd2-bf7a-c9dfffda49a0" :answer/id #uuid "27570a30-bc64-5dd2-bf7a-c9dfffda49a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e970e43-56e8-51a7-8b67-1a34c8189d31" :answer/id #uuid "3e970e43-56e8-51a7-8b67-1a34c8189d31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z=-2,4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-654e9075-d5c0-5942-8335-3af4eecc1069" :answer/id #uuid "654e9075-d5c0-5942-8335-3af4eecc1069" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03a4f702-63d6-559a-a0dd-ab624a5eb606" :answer/id #uuid "03a4f702-63d6-559a-a0dd-ab624a5eb606" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-609ca3c1-35ba-50ca-a3f5-8ffaec27d898" :answer/id #uuid "609ca3c1-35ba-50ca-a3f5-8ffaec27d898" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The equation has no real solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054741 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-11794345-6933-57d1-83b3-9cd504769483"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-11794345-6933-57d1-83b3-9cd504769483" :question/id #uuid "11794345-6933-57d1-83b3-9cd504769483" :question/math-academy-id "q-113152" :question/problem "Find the roots of $f(t)=\\frac{3t-9}{{t}^{2}+t-12}.$" :question/worked-solution "The roots of $f(t)$ are the solutions to the equation $f(t)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{3t-9}{{t}^{2}+t-12}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{3t-9}{{t}^{2}+t-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(t-3)}{(t-3)(t+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cancel{(t-3)}}{\\cancel{(t-3)}(t+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the numerator equal to zero, we get $3=0,$ which is a false statement. Therefore, $f(t)=0$ has no solution, and so $f(t)$ has no roots." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ce072ed5-fff1-5f57-8574-8b1467c81f11"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ce072ed5-fff1-5f57-8574-8b1467c81f11" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce072ed5-fff1-5f57-8574-8b1467c81f11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7fe183f1-adc3-55ca-a0f5-2cb7c6c206e7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0c6081c3-38a0-553f-95b3-bb1b79615863" "answer-94e6af2f-d3d4-5aa3-96b5-dd921249cb1b" "answer-9df9d439-2cc7-5118-ac3a-b34455eb4e49" "answer-804cb341-7dd4-5100-bc89-462928a3d075" "answer-7fe183f1-adc3-55ca-a0f5-2cb7c6c206e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c6081c3-38a0-553f-95b3-bb1b79615863" :answer/id #uuid "0c6081c3-38a0-553f-95b3-bb1b79615863" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94e6af2f-d3d4-5aa3-96b5-dd921249cb1b" :answer/id #uuid "94e6af2f-d3d4-5aa3-96b5-dd921249cb1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9df9d439-2cc7-5118-ac3a-b34455eb4e49" :answer/id #uuid "9df9d439-2cc7-5118-ac3a-b34455eb4e49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-804cb341-7dd4-5100-bc89-462928a3d075" :answer/id #uuid "804cb341-7dd4-5100-bc89-462928a3d075" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fe183f1-adc3-55ca-a0f5-2cb7c6c206e7" :answer/id #uuid "7fe183f1-adc3-55ca-a0f5-2cb7c6c206e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No roots" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054742 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-87c77350-058a-500e-bb8c-ed314494ab76"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-87c77350-058a-500e-bb8c-ed314494ab76" :question/id #uuid "87c77350-058a-500e-bb8c-ed314494ab76" :question/math-academy-id "q-113168" :question/problem "Solve $g(y)=0$ where $g(y)=\\frac{{y}^{3}-2{y}^{2}-25y+50}{{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}-25y-25}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y) & =\\frac{{y}^{3}-2{y}^{2}-25y+50}{{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}-25y-25} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}(y-2)-25(y-2)}{{y}^{2}(y+1)-25(y+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-2)({y}^{2}-25)}{(y+1)({y}^{2}-25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-2)(y-5)(y+5)}{(y+1)(y-5)(y+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-2)\\cancel{(y-5)(y+5)}}{(y+1)\\cancel{(y-5)(y+5)}} &  \\\\  & =\\frac{y-2}{y+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2=0\\,⟹\\,y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to $g(y)=0$ is $y=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4500e6f6-1360-506a-b80c-23e6f852f50c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4500e6f6-1360-506a-b80c-23e6f852f50c" :answer-field/id #uuid "4500e6f6-1360-506a-b80c-23e6f852f50c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3035b6f7-dba3-5eb6-b785-1c9139483a03" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bba86845-7980-5a1c-8edf-d8acd2d217eb" "answer-09e45747-6dab-55e5-a4ac-42f5c054c709" "answer-3035b6f7-dba3-5eb6-b785-1c9139483a03" "answer-9ea0ed19-e8bc-5906-af88-d2c141f8e4a9" "answer-55c97b34-1a0c-5fa1-a935-7a5e02916c64"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bba86845-7980-5a1c-8edf-d8acd2d217eb" :answer/id #uuid "bba86845-7980-5a1c-8edf-d8acd2d217eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09e45747-6dab-55e5-a4ac-42f5c054c709" :answer/id #uuid "09e45747-6dab-55e5-a4ac-42f5c054c709" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-5,\\,5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3035b6f7-dba3-5eb6-b785-1c9139483a03" :answer/id #uuid "3035b6f7-dba3-5eb6-b785-1c9139483a03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ea0ed19-e8bc-5906-af88-d2c141f8e4a9" :answer/id #uuid "9ea0ed19-e8bc-5906-af88-d2c141f8e4a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2,\\,5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55c97b34-1a0c-5fa1-a935-7a5e02916c64" :answer/id #uuid "55c97b34-1a0c-5fa1-a935-7a5e02916c64" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The equation has no solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-8ba659a1-95fd-5dbf-977d-e7f98db61b34" :activity/id #uuid "8ba659a1-95fd-5dbf-977d-e7f98db61b34" :activity/title "Roots of Rational Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049931 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-42b328a5-8ffd-5876-afdc-e5a592324737" "step-268eb4f9-3381-592c-aa6f-741cf272b0a8" "step-ea2da595-f24a-5651-9567-526c9ece20d8"] :activity/first-step "step-42b328a5-8ffd-5876-afdc-e5a592324737" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-42b328a5-8ffd-5876-afdc-e5a592324737" :step/id #uuid "42b328a5-8ffd-5876-afdc-e5a592324737" :step/content "question-afbc25b8-e022-51c1-8f23-554ddf441b28" :step/next "step-268eb4f9-3381-592c-aa6f-741cf272b0a8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-268eb4f9-3381-592c-aa6f-741cf272b0a8" :step/id #uuid "268eb4f9-3381-592c-aa6f-741cf272b0a8" :step/content "question-11794345-6933-57d1-83b3-9cd504769483" :step/next "step-ea2da595-f24a-5651-9567-526c9ece20d8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ea2da595-f24a-5651-9567-526c9ece20d8" :step/id #uuid "ea2da595-f24a-5651-9567-526c9ece20d8" :step/content "question-87c77350-058a-500e-bb8c-ed314494ab76" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186164787 :answer-field/choices 17592186164788] [:db/retract 17592186164787 :answer-field/choices 17592186164789] [:db/retract 17592186164787 :answer-field/choices 17592186164790] [:db/retract 17592186164787 :answer-field/choices 17592186164791] [:db/retract 17592186164792 :answer-field/choices 17592186164793] [:db/retract 17592186164792 :answer-field/choices 17592186164794] [:db/retract 17592186164792 :answer-field/choices 17592186164795] [:db/retract 17592186164792 :answer-field/choices 17592186164796] [:db/retract 17592186314274 :answer-field/choices 17592186313946] [:db/retract 17592186314472 :answer-field/choices 17592186312788] [:db/retract 17592186314430 :answer-field/choices 17592186313730] {:db/id 17592186166940 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e119a1c3-3fb4-5a0d-b097-eb9b2cba2dd5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167267 :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$  {{field-1}}, where $n\\in {N}_{0}.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not a parameter of the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+4\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 2\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n2B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{\\lambda }=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi =\\frac{(1+2n)\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in {N}_{0}.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164787 :answer-field/correct "answer-5f820d1b-c322-595d-b365-4644c6d4d4b6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5f820d1b-c322-595d-b365-4644c6d4d4b6" "answer-463598a2-802d-5891-9146-cf86fa6b8eb0" "answer-568b0e44-93e7-5f72-ae24-3f002855e7b7" "answer-f768356d-54ca-54ba-94ac-a515ad1c7618"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f820d1b-c322-595d-b365-4644c6d4d4b6" :answer/id #uuid "5f820d1b-c322-595d-b365-4644c6d4d4b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-463598a2-802d-5891-9146-cf86fa6b8eb0" :answer/id #uuid "463598a2-802d-5891-9146-cf86fa6b8eb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(1+4n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-568b0e44-93e7-5f72-ae24-3f002855e7b7" :answer/id #uuid "568b0e44-93e7-5f72-ae24-3f002855e7b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f768356d-54ca-54ba-94ac-a515ad1c7618" :answer/id #uuid "f768356d-54ca-54ba-94ac-a515ad1c7618" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164792 :answer-field/correct "answer-825a9c42-aa52-56eb-a2d0-d8428c2fac85" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ba16303c-d7ba-5f9a-8c48-d91e998775c0" "answer-1a4dd299-25b5-5ac9-8cdd-da41260030e6" "answer-825a9c42-aa52-56eb-a2d0-d8428c2fac85" "answer-fa306cfd-ae11-5cd7-a248-0203ef2f5d10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba16303c-d7ba-5f9a-8c48-d91e998775c0" :answer/id #uuid "ba16303c-d7ba-5f9a-8c48-d91e998775c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a4dd299-25b5-5ac9-8cdd-da41260030e6" :answer/id #uuid "1a4dd299-25b5-5ac9-8cdd-da41260030e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-825a9c42-aa52-56eb-a2d0-d8428c2fac85" :answer/id #uuid "825a9c42-aa52-56eb-a2d0-d8428c2fac85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa306cfd-ae11-5cd7-a248-0203ef2f5d10" :answer/id #uuid "fa306cfd-ae11-5cd7-a248-0203ef2f5d10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166939 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d3e4d429-39a4-5565-a491-a0dbab9cb6bc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d3e4d429-39a4-5565-a491-a0dbab9cb6bc" :question/id #uuid "d3e4d429-39a4-5565-a491-a0dbab9cb6bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338898" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-12\\lambda {y}^{′}+36{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+6{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions.\n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$\n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+6{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-12\\lambda m+36{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-6\\lambda )(m-6\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=6\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{6\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{6\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda x}+B{e}^{6\\lambda x}+6B\\lambda x{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 6A\\lambda +B+6B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+6{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{12\\lambda }+2B{e}^{12\\lambda }+6(6A\\lambda {e}^{12\\lambda }+B{e}^{12\\lambda }+12B\\lambda {e}^{12\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+6(6A\\lambda +B+12B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+36A\\lambda +6B+72B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+36\\lambda )A+(8+72\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+36\\lambda )A+8(1+9\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+36\\lambda )A+(8+72\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda  & 1+6\\lambda  \\\\ 1+36\\lambda  & 8+72\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda (8+72\\lambda )-(1+6\\lambda )(1+36\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 48\\lambda +432{\\lambda }^{2}-(1+42\\lambda +216{\\lambda }^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 216{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (12\\lambda +1)(18\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{12}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{18}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{12}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x/2}+Bx{e}^{-x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{1}{2}B=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=B,$ where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =B{e}^{-x/2}+Bx{e}^{-x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{-x/2}(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{18},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/3}+Bx{e}^{x/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}A+\\frac{4}{3}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-4B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-4B{e}^{x/3}+Bx{e}^{x/3} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/3}(x-4) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/3}(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x/2}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/3}(x-4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2f5d72b2-1ac6-56f0-b377-5ca377210957" "answer-field-8cec229b-c780-5301-8ffe-447f6b02d795"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2f5d72b2-1ac6-56f0-b377-5ca377210957" :answer-field/id #uuid "2f5d72b2-1ac6-56f0-b377-5ca377210957" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-a5d43ac0-9269-5f2e-ac1f-07ceda1132ce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-db16e7ff-5f98-57eb-80cb-369c7c0fc1d1" "answer-18495168-a2f2-5760-9658-64137996b12e" "answer-a5d43ac0-9269-5f2e-ac1f-07ceda1132ce" "answer-38ad86d5-fe25-58e4-90bc-5eac3ba443d3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db16e7ff-5f98-57eb-80cb-369c7c0fc1d1" :answer/id #uuid "db16e7ff-5f98-57eb-80cb-369c7c0fc1d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18495168-a2f2-5760-9658-64137996b12e" :answer/id #uuid "18495168-a2f2-5760-9658-64137996b12e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5d43ac0-9269-5f2e-ac1f-07ceda1132ce" :answer/id #uuid "a5d43ac0-9269-5f2e-ac1f-07ceda1132ce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{12}$ and $\\frac{1}{18}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38ad86d5-fe25-58e4-90bc-5eac3ba443d3" :answer/id #uuid "38ad86d5-fe25-58e4-90bc-5eac3ba443d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{18}$ and $\\frac{1}{12}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8cec229b-c780-5301-8ffe-447f6b02d795" :answer-field/id #uuid "8cec229b-c780-5301-8ffe-447f6b02d795" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-b0bbc9f2-f65d-50b3-8c20-aa6c05a8948c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b0bbc9f2-f65d-50b3-8c20-aa6c05a8948c" "answer-619c5499-6599-5620-83f9-56cce4f5fab6" "answer-e537a154-19fd-52b4-8885-cfa1f1c64a64" "answer-a0d1ea79-2eda-572c-80ad-221ef3734d49"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0bbc9f2-f65d-50b3-8c20-aa6c05a8948c" :answer/id #uuid "b0bbc9f2-f65d-50b3-8c20-aa6c05a8948c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x/2}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/3}(x-4)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-619c5499-6599-5620-83f9-56cce4f5fab6" :answer/id #uuid "619c5499-6599-5620-83f9-56cce4f5fab6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x/2}(x-1)$ and $C{e}^{x/3}(x+4)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e537a154-19fd-52b4-8885-cfa1f1c64a64" :answer/id #uuid "e537a154-19fd-52b4-8885-cfa1f1c64a64" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-4)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0d1ea79-2eda-572c-80ad-221ef3734d49" :answer/id #uuid "a0d1ea79-2eda-572c-80ad-221ef3734d49" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B{e}^{x/2}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{-x/3}(x-4)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186314743 :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16\\lambda {y}^{′}+64{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-16\\lambda m+64{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-8\\lambda )(m-8\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=8\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{8\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{8\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{8\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda x}+B{e}^{8\\lambda x}+8B\\lambda x{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (8\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(8A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+16A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(8\\lambda )A+B=0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+16\\lambda  & 3+16\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda (3+16\\lambda )-(1)(1+16\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 24\\lambda +128{\\lambda }^{2}-1-16\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 128{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (16\\lambda -1)(8\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186314274 :answer-field/correct 17592186312328 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186314472 :answer-field/correct 17592186313814 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-e119a1c3-3fb4-5a0d-b097-eb9b2cba2dd5" :question/id #uuid "e119a1c3-3fb4-5a0d-b097-eb9b2cba2dd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-338911" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+25{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 5\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n5B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\lambda )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n10\\lambda =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi =\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{20}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-332d2f8f-47d7-550b-9380-93ef85d9ab1c" "answer-field-f2b1e7ca-994b-5508-b638-3a8f99c45170"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-332d2f8f-47d7-550b-9380-93ef85d9ab1c" :answer-field/id #uuid "332d2f8f-47d7-550b-9380-93ef85d9ab1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-d7c85d4a-dd82-53d1-bf3d-6b0ac4ea8760" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3a4f7c24-e0b9-5bf3-9e4d-a25f296ccb48" "answer-d7c85d4a-dd82-53d1-bf3d-6b0ac4ea8760" "answer-6df027d0-2009-5803-b6ab-84d40381f957" "answer-d996cdfc-0e79-5226-8c9a-877b887a3bf5" "answer-67ff3321-c036-5b29-9dad-55a412690dac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a4f7c24-e0b9-5bf3-9e4d-a25f296ccb48" :answer/id #uuid "3a4f7c24-e0b9-5bf3-9e4d-a25f296ccb48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7c85d4a-dd82-53d1-bf3d-6b0ac4ea8760" :answer/id #uuid "d7c85d4a-dd82-53d1-bf3d-6b0ac4ea8760" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6df027d0-2009-5803-b6ab-84d40381f957" :answer/id #uuid "6df027d0-2009-5803-b6ab-84d40381f957" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d996cdfc-0e79-5226-8c9a-877b887a3bf5" :answer/id #uuid "d996cdfc-0e79-5226-8c9a-877b887a3bf5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67ff3321-c036-5b29-9dad-55a412690dac" :answer/id #uuid "67ff3321-c036-5b29-9dad-55a412690dac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f2b1e7ca-994b-5508-b638-3a8f99c45170" :answer-field/id #uuid "f2b1e7ca-994b-5508-b638-3a8f99c45170" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-41cc2c8c-3160-5bbd-8cc4-960e5fbda21e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a998a8d9-6a09-5d04-acb0-872ca47788d9" "answer-fed8e2de-30fd-518a-ac16-176c89ef2fc2" "answer-1d235ca0-01c3-55ec-8fcb-222232223b0d" "answer-41cc2c8c-3160-5bbd-8cc4-960e5fbda21e" "answer-5389d731-66ed-5d65-93ef-3a80e24757d2" "answer-9caaa3b1-2fd7-5093-989a-4a1ad46a3f24"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a998a8d9-6a09-5d04-acb0-872ca47788d9" :answer/id #uuid "a998a8d9-6a09-5d04-acb0-872ca47788d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fed8e2de-30fd-518a-ac16-176c89ef2fc2" :answer/id #uuid "fed8e2de-30fd-518a-ac16-176c89ef2fc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5{\\lambda }_{n}x}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d235ca0-01c3-55ec-8fcb-222232223b0d" :answer/id #uuid "1d235ca0-01c3-55ec-8fcb-222232223b0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41cc2c8c-3160-5bbd-8cc4-960e5fbda21e" :answer/id #uuid "41cc2c8c-3160-5bbd-8cc4-960e5fbda21e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5389d731-66ed-5d65-93ef-3a80e24757d2" :answer/id #uuid "5389d731-66ed-5d65-93ef-3a80e24757d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{\\lambda }_{n}x}{5})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9caaa3b1-2fd7-5093-989a-4a1ad46a3f24" :answer/id #uuid "9caaa3b1-2fd7-5093-989a-4a1ad46a3f24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186314746 :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25\\lambda y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186314430 :answer-field/correct 17592186312166 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "activity-733c7dd3-213c-5216-b0fe-5cc93bd162c8" :activity/id #uuid "733c7dd3-213c-5216-b0fe-5cc93bd162c8" :activity/title "Eigenvalues and Eigenfunctions of Homogeneous BVPs" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049830 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-9413bf48-d935-5135-a6f0-348e7d65f04d" "step-4affdd5b-9eb0-5e02-a8fc-15da98d24def" "step-b7a36c8c-b03f-5f19-ac0b-d15bd0a7bd0a" "step-2a58e732-9b94-5f3d-88a9-7a4d9823485f" "step-843f985f-228b-50fa-8fb3-bdfb81168ccb"] :activity/first-step "step-9413bf48-d935-5135-a6f0-348e7d65f04d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-9413bf48-d935-5135-a6f0-348e7d65f04d" :step/id #uuid "9413bf48-d935-5135-a6f0-348e7d65f04d" :step/content 17592186167267 :step/next "step-4affdd5b-9eb0-5e02-a8fc-15da98d24def" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4affdd5b-9eb0-5e02-a8fc-15da98d24def" :step/id #uuid "4affdd5b-9eb0-5e02-a8fc-15da98d24def" :step/content "question-d3e4d429-39a4-5565-a491-a0dbab9cb6bc" :step/next "step-b7a36c8c-b03f-5f19-ac0b-d15bd0a7bd0a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b7a36c8c-b03f-5f19-ac0b-d15bd0a7bd0a" :step/id #uuid "b7a36c8c-b03f-5f19-ac0b-d15bd0a7bd0a" :step/content 17592186314743 :step/next "step-2a58e732-9b94-5f3d-88a9-7a4d9823485f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2a58e732-9b94-5f3d-88a9-7a4d9823485f" :step/id #uuid "2a58e732-9b94-5f3d-88a9-7a4d9823485f" :step/content "question-e119a1c3-3fb4-5a0d-b097-eb9b2cba2dd5" :step/next "step-843f985f-228b-50fa-8fb3-bdfb81168ccb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-843f985f-228b-50fa-8fb3-bdfb81168ccb" :step/id #uuid "843f985f-228b-50fa-8fb3-bdfb81168ccb" :step/content 17592186314746 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054744 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7501f09d-5828-597b-9243-c1ed53d4a63e" "question-834c413e-3660-5a12-aa09-b30d7cd9a51f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054541 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\log x ?$ \n\nI. $\\dfrac {\\log_{2} x} {\\log_{2} 10}$\n\nII. $\\dfrac {\\log_{2} 10} {\\log_{2} x}$\n\nIII. $\\log_{2}\\left(\\dfrac{x}{10}\\right)$" :question/worked-solution "The change of base formula for logarithms is\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}.\n$$\n\nThe change of base formula works with any new base. In this example, the choices are written using base $2,$ so we rewrite $\\log x$ in terms of $\\log_2.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡x & ={log}_{10}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{{log}_{2}⁡10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054745 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4c277570-c608-5c8a-9eec-65493369dacb" "question-fd36bb1c-2d38-5fe5-b95b-ae2bbaecd296"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054543 :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate $\\log_3 (7)$ by changing the base $3$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=3,$ $X =7,$ and $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(7) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(7)}{{log}_{10}⁡(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.845\\,10}{0.477\\,12} \\\\  & ≈1.77.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054540 :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=16$ and $X =32.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2,$ we set $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{16}⁡(32) & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(32)}{{log}_{2}⁡(16)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{5})}{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-7501f09d-5828-597b-9243-c1ed53d4a63e" :question/id #uuid "7501f09d-5828-597b-9243-c1ed53d4a63e" :question/math-academy-id "q-262939" :question/problem "If ${log}_{125}⁡x=\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{k},$ where $k$ is an integer, then $k=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=125$ to base $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{125}⁡x & =\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{{log}_{5}⁡125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the expression in the denominator using exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{{log}_{5}⁡125} & =\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{{log}_{5}⁡({5}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{log}_{125}⁡x=\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, $k=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cc3e8381-6b4f-5e93-ae04-8cf9545b1218"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cc3e8381-6b4f-5e93-ae04-8cf9545b1218" :answer-field/id #uuid "cc3e8381-6b4f-5e93-ae04-8cf9545b1218" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-42c12419-a232-5cd2-b235-39a6a1843fb6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-42c12419-a232-5cd2-b235-39a6a1843fb6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42c12419-a232-5cd2-b235-39a6a1843fb6" :answer/id #uuid "42c12419-a232-5cd2-b235-39a6a1843fb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-834c413e-3660-5a12-aa09-b30d7cd9a51f" :question/id #uuid "834c413e-3660-5a12-aa09-b30d7cd9a51f" :question/math-academy-id "q-262839" :question/problem "Let $\\operatorname{log}⁡x=t.$ Express ${log}_{1000}⁡x$ via the variable $t.$\n\n${log}_{1000}⁡x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=1000$ to base $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{1000}⁡x & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{{log}_{10}⁡(1000)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the expression in the denominator using exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{{log}_{10}⁡(1000)} & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{{log}_{10}⁡({10}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{log}⁡x=t,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{log}_{1000}⁡x=\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{3}=\\frac{t}{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e5c30e83-ea24-50fa-94a2-e0cd96a4bf88"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e5c30e83-ea24-50fa-94a2-e0cd96a4bf88" :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c30e83-ea24-50fa-94a2-e0cd96a4bf88" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f05b9105-6358-56c2-af8c-ebeaec8564d6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f05b9105-6358-56c2-af8c-ebeaec8564d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f05b9105-6358-56c2-af8c-ebeaec8564d6" :answer/id #uuid "f05b9105-6358-56c2-af8c-ebeaec8564d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{t}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4c277570-c608-5c8a-9eec-65493369dacb" :question/id #uuid "4c277570-c608-5c8a-9eec-65493369dacb" :question/math-academy-id "q-108372" :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{11}⁡(7)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{11}⁡(7)$ by changing the base $11$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=11,$ $X=7,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{11}⁡(7) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(7)}{{log}_{10}⁡(11)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.845\\,098}{1.041\\,393} \\\\  & ≈0.812\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e017985b-c529-50e2-9421-397fcbb26ef9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e017985b-c529-50e2-9421-397fcbb26ef9" :answer-field/id #uuid "e017985b-c529-50e2-9421-397fcbb26ef9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-13b65618-43ee-532e-a839-a31e265d785c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-202305ae-992e-5dff-bc4e-bc45335f1658" "answer-b3c52165-df02-5476-941b-b3846aa44552" "answer-13b65618-43ee-532e-a839-a31e265d785c" "answer-6d30e107-e689-5ca8-ade5-ea5a816ad541" "answer-180aac80-cd6c-553f-9390-bd5447a64e03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-202305ae-992e-5dff-bc4e-bc45335f1658" :answer/id #uuid "202305ae-992e-5dff-bc4e-bc45335f1658" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.714" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3c52165-df02-5476-941b-b3846aa44552" :answer/id #uuid "b3c52165-df02-5476-941b-b3846aa44552" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.765" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13b65618-43ee-532e-a839-a31e265d785c" :answer/id #uuid "13b65618-43ee-532e-a839-a31e265d785c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.812" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d30e107-e689-5ca8-ade5-ea5a816ad541" :answer/id #uuid "6d30e107-e689-5ca8-ade5-ea5a816ad541" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.893" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-180aac80-cd6c-553f-9390-bd5447a64e03" :answer/id #uuid "180aac80-cd6c-553f-9390-bd5447a64e03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.917" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fd36bb1c-2d38-5fe5-b95b-ae2bbaecd296" :question/id #uuid "fd36bb1c-2d38-5fe5-b95b-ae2bbaecd296" :question/math-academy-id "q-262883" :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{8}⁡(19)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n${log}_{8}⁡(19)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{8}⁡(19)$ by changing the base $8$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ $X=19,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡(19) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(19)}{{log}_{10}⁡(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(19)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1.278\\,754}{0.903\\,090} \\\\  & ≈1.416\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-99060676-2b65-517f-864d-a46840395897"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-99060676-2b65-517f-864d-a46840395897" :answer-field/id #uuid "99060676-2b65-517f-864d-a46840395897" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e53d2060-f4c9-588d-b716-6b6725d895f9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e53d2060-f4c9-588d-b716-6b6725d895f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e53d2060-f4c9-588d-b716-6b6725d895f9" :answer/id #uuid "e53d2060-f4c9-588d-b716-6b6725d895f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.416" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055183 :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, ${log}_{16}⁡(64)$ equals{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=16$ and $X=64.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2,$ we set $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{16}⁡(64) & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(64)}{{log}_{2}⁡(16)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{6})}{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186052384 :answer-field/correct "answer-ca66def7-3c65-5210-99d9-f0db68c4888c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ca66def7-3c65-5210-99d9-f0db68c4888c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca66def7-3c65-5210-99d9-f0db68c4888c" :answer/id #uuid "ca66def7-3c65-5210-99d9-f0db68c4888c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055462 :question/problem "${log}_{36}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6})=$" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=36$ and $X=\\sqrt[3]{6}.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ can be presented as a power of $6,$ we set $b=6.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{36}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6}) & =\\frac{{log}_{6}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6})}{{log}_{6}⁡(36)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}⁡({6}^{1/3})}{{log}_{6}⁡({6}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054009 :answer-field/correct 17592186054014 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055970 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to evaluate $\\log_{4}9,$ but our calculator only has a key for the common logarithm $\\log$ (base $10$).\n\nIn such cases, we can use the **change of base formula**, which states that\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}.\n$$\n\nIt effectively allows us to change a logarithm from one base, $a,$ to another, $b.$ Note that $X,$ $a,$ and $b$ have to be all greater than zero and that $a \\ne 1,$ $b \\ne 1.$ \n\nWe usually choose the value of base $b$ that makes the problem easier. In particular, if we need to evaluate the expression using a calculator, we choose $b=10$ or $b=e$. \n\nFor example, to evaluate $\\log_{4}9$ using this formula, we can rewrite the logarithm from base $a=4$ to base $b=10.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡9 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡9}{\\operatorname{log}⁡4} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.954\\,242}{0.602\\,059} \\\\  & =1.585\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054890 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186317392 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 3, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\pi .$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\pi .$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186317394 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166961 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b0fd3313-c314-58d3-a500-207bbe04cd58"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186402284 :question/problem "Consider the function $F(x)=|x|$ defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}(2n-1)^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2}x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b0fd3313-c314-58d3-a500-207bbe04cd58" :question/id #uuid "b0fd3313-c314-58d3-a500-207bbe04cd58" :question/math-academy-id "q-343403" :question/problem "Consider the function $F(x)=x$ defined on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-3}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-5}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we separate the Fourier series into its odd and even terms as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)∼2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)x)-2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1}-2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n}\\cdot 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d1467dc5-08ea-5550-9843-e23f5fff69d8" "answer-field-ccf42bc2-7de5-54af-91ad-b973a99aeb58" "answer-field-2f347663-10f1-5532-a12b-d4da98b820ac" "answer-field-38a73ecb-974e-5f13-bd82-ea43d6ba0541" "answer-field-29458fa7-bc42-566e-b9be-960433c46a54"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d1467dc5-08ea-5550-9843-e23f5fff69d8" :answer-field/id #uuid "d1467dc5-08ea-5550-9843-e23f5fff69d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-c18abbb0-d30b-551c-9178-0ec9bf716e5c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c18abbb0-d30b-551c-9178-0ec9bf716e5c" "answer-66779975-74ee-50cf-a35c-645d398b77e2" "answer-e69ac2ef-dd0e-5e72-9a1f-61bf0d3b2e89" "answer-b5aa77fe-dec0-5160-8a2e-1b65c8cb3c51"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c18abbb0-d30b-551c-9178-0ec9bf716e5c" :answer/id #uuid "c18abbb0-d30b-551c-9178-0ec9bf716e5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66779975-74ee-50cf-a35c-645d398b77e2" :answer/id #uuid "66779975-74ee-50cf-a35c-645d398b77e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e69ac2ef-dd0e-5e72-9a1f-61bf0d3b2e89" :answer/id #uuid "e69ac2ef-dd0e-5e72-9a1f-61bf0d3b2e89" :answer/typ